  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-115938",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point:  \n\n$\\,$ $x=-1$  \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. Consider the vertical line $x=-1$ and choose an arbitrary point on it.\n\n$\\,$ So, we draw it as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/9062dcd04f6ca0dcb21453596ebd007ccf91a33f5092a601d24e401e888a20f1.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and increasing $↗$ on $(-1,\\infty )$, the graph of $f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=-1.$\n\n- Moreover, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing to the left of $x=2$ and to the right of $x=2$, although with different slopes, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)>0.$ So, the curve $y=f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,2)$  and on $(2,\\infty ).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/fbd0da8fbd98e0b83cd1efb82d5e1f1aca1bf40108e11c6a401da46e514a133a.png)\n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/95cc3ce5bab09a3e3ea32571e2156d13605b85bf8f026c7c35b5dd51d045c7fd.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
